course: books
lesson: 8
title: Alice in Wonderland, chapter IV, part 2: Little Bill, the pebbles and the puppy
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Outside the house a whole crowd arrives with ladders, and after a lot of shouting they decide that poor Bill, a lizard, must climb down the chimney to deal with the giant.
explain: Alice gives one sharp kick with the foot she has up the chimney, and Bill shoots out "like a sky-rocket"; when the Rabbit talks of burning the house down, Alice threatens to set her cat Dinah on them.
explain: Next they throw pebbles in at the window, but the pebbles turn into little cakes; Alice eats one, shrinks, and runs past the crowd into a thick wood.
explain: There a puppy, enormous to tiny Alice, bounds at her; she keeps it busy with a stick, dodging round a thistle, until it gets tired and she escapes.
explain: Resting, she wonders what to eat or drink to grow again, and peeps over a big mushroom to find a large blue caterpillar sitting on top: noisy and slapstick first, then quiet and curious.
fact: Alice hears a rumbling of little cartwheels and many voices: "Where's the other ladder?—Why, I hadn't to bring but one; Bill's got the other".
fact: They tie the ladders together, because one alone does not "reach half high enough"; a loose slate (a flat stone tile of the roof) falls with a crash, and someone shouts "Heads below!", which means watch out below.
fact: Nobody wants to go down the chimney, so it is "Bill's to go down"; Alice says "they seem to put everything upon Bill!", meaning he gets all the jobs nobody wants.
fact: Alice draws her foot down the chimney, waits till she hears a little animal scrambling close above her, says "This is Bill," and gives "one sharp kick" to stop it; a chorus cries "There goes Bill!"
fact: Bill, feeble and squeaky, says "something comes at me like a Jack-in-the-box, and up I goes like a sky-rocket!"; a Jack-in-the-box is a toy figure that springs out of a box, and a sky-rocket is a firework.
fact: The Rabbit says "We must burn the house down!", and Alice shouts "If you do, I'll set Dinah at you!", after which there is "a dead silence".
fact: Alice thinks "If they had any sense, they'd take the roof off."
fact: The Rabbit orders "A barrowful will do, to begin with", meaning a wheelbarrow full, and a shower of pebbles rattles in at the window.
fact: The pebbles turn into little cakes, and Alice reasons that a cake "can't possibly make me larger", so it "must make me smaller, I suppose".
fact: She shrinks, runs out, and sees Bill the Lizard being held up by two guinea-pigs, who give him something out of a bottle; the crowd rushes at her, but she escapes into a thick wood.
fact: Her plan is first "to grow to my right size again" and second "to find my way into that lovely garden", but "she had not the smallest idea how to set about it".
fact: An enormous puppy reaches out a paw to her; Alice speaks to it "in a coaxing tone" (gently and sweetly), but she is afraid it might be hungry and eat her.
fact: She holds out a stick, and the puppy rushes at it and "worries" it (shakes and bites at it); Alice dodges behind a thistle, a prickly plant, and feels it is like playing with a cart-horse.
fact: At last the puppy sits panting with its tongue out, and Alice runs off and leans against a buttercup to rest; later she says "what a dear little puppy it was!"
fact: She looks under the big mushroom, on both sides of it and behind it, and then thinks of looking on the top: there, on top of the mushroom, as tall as she is, sits a large blue caterpillar "with its arms folded, quietly smoking a long hookah"; a hookah is a tall smoking pipe with water in it, and smoking is very bad for health.
fact: Old words in today's English: "I fancy" = I think, "don't be particular" = don't be fussy, "a deal too flustered" = much too upset and muddled, "made believe" = pretended.
exercise: What sound told Alice that more creatures had arrived? | A rumbling of little cartwheels and many voices | A loud trumpet and the beating of a big drum | The White Rabbit ringing a bell at the gate | Dinah meowing loudly outside the front door
exercise: Who had the other ladder? | Bill | Pat, the gardener | The Rabbit | Mary Ann
exercise: Why did they tie the ladders together? | One ladder alone did not reach high enough | The ladders were broken and needed mending | They wanted to make a bridge over the pond | The Rabbit said it looked much tidier that way
exercise: What fell from the roof with a loud crash? | A loose slate | The chimney | A ladder | Bill the Lizard himself
exercise: Here "Heads below!" means... | Watch out below! | Count everybody's heads! | Bend your heads down low! | Look up at the sky!
exercise: Who was sent down the chimney? | Bill | Pat, the gardener | The Rabbit | A guinea-pig
exercise: What did Alice mean by "they seem to put everything upon Bill"? | Bill gets all the jobs nobody else wants | Bill is the strongest and biggest of them all | They keep putting heavy boxes on Bill's back | Bill is the one who always tells them what to do
exercise: What did Alice do when Bill came down the chimney? | Gave one sharp kick | Pulled him out gently | Blew him back up the chimney | Shouted "Boo!" at him
exercise: What did the crowd cry out when Bill flew up? | "There goes Bill!" | "Heads below!" | "Catch the giant!" | "Off with his head!"
exercise: What did Bill say he went up like? | A sky-rocket | A big balloon | A little bird | A kite in the wind
exercise: What is a Jack-in-the-box? | A toy figure that springs up out of a box | A boy who hides in a cupboard all day | A big box that is used for carrying ladders | A kind of firework that sparkles in the sky
exercise: What did the Rabbit say they must do to the house? | Burn it down | Paint it white | Knock on the door | Pull the chimney down
exercise: How did Alice stop the Rabbit's plan? | She threatened to set Dinah at them | She promised to come out quietly | She asked the Mouse to help her | She started to cry so loudly that they ran off
exercise: What did Alice think they should do, if they had any sense? | Take the roof off | Climb in the window | Wait till the morning | Call the Duchess for help
exercise: Here "a barrowful" means... | As much as fills a wheelbarrow | A whole bucket full of water | A load carried on a donkey | A heap as high as the roof
exercise: What came rattling in at the window? | A shower of little pebbles | A shower of apples | A swarm of bees | A bucket of cold water from the pond
exercise: What happened to the pebbles on the floor? | They turned into little cakes | They turned into golden keys | They melted into a pool of water | They hopped about like frogs
exercise: Why did Alice think a pebble-cake would make her smaller? | It could not possibly make her any larger | It had "EAT ME" written on it in currants | The Rabbit told her it would make her smaller | She had eaten one before and it shrank her
exercise: Who was holding Bill up when Alice came out? | Two guinea-pigs | Pat and the Rabbit | The Dodo and the Duck | Two little mice
exercise: Where did Alice run to be safe? | Into a thick wood | Back to the pool | Up a tall tree | Into the lovely garden at last
exercise: What were the two parts of Alice's plan in the wood? | Grow to her right size, then find the lovely garden | Find the Rabbit, then give him back his gloves | Go home to Dinah, then tell her sister everything | Catch the puppy, then teach it some tricks
exercise: What was the only difficulty with Alice's plan? | She had no idea how to set about it | The Rabbit was chasing her through the wood | It was getting far too dark to see | The garden door had been taken away
exercise: Why was Alice frightened of the puppy? | It might be hungry and eat her up | It was barking and growling at her | It had very long sharp claws and teeth | It belonged to the angry Duchess
exercise: What did Alice hold out to the puppy? | A little stick | A comfit | A glove | Her own silver thimble
exercise: What did Alice hide behind to dodge the puppy? | A great thistle | A big mushroom | A tree stump | A buttercup as tall as a tree
exercise: What did playing with the puppy feel like to Alice? | Like playing with a cart-horse | Like riding on the Dodo's back | Like swimming in the pool of tears | Like running in the Caucus-race
exercise: What did Alice lean against to rest? | A buttercup | A thistle | A mushroom | A fence at the edge of the wood
exercise: What was sitting on top of the mushroom? | A large blue caterpillar | A small green frog | The White Rabbit | A cat grinning from ear to ear
exercise: Thinking back: who is Pat, whom we met earlier in this chapter? | The Rabbit's gardener, "digging for apples" | The Rabbit's housemaid, who is called Mary Ann | A guinea-pig who held up Bill after his fall | The Dodo's cousin, who joined the Caucus-race
exercise: Thinking back: how did the birds react in chapter III when Alice talked about Dinah? | They all made excuses and went home | They asked Alice to bring Dinah to them | They laughed and said cats can't fly | They gave Dinah a prize for catching mice
test: Ethan goes: "Alice kicked Bill 'cos she hates lizards." Put him right. | She kicked to stop whatever was coming down the chimney | She kicked because Bill had been rude to her earlier | She kicked by accident while she was fast asleep | She kicked because Dinah had told her to do it
test: Chloe, Gen Z: "Poor Bill is literally the group project guy, they dump everything on him." ("group project guy" = the one who ends up doing all the work.) Which line from Alice says the same? | "they seem to put everything upon Bill!" | "Bill! fetch it here, lad!" | "Here, Bill! catch hold of this rope" | "There goes Bill!"
test: Why did the Rabbit's crowd go silent when Alice mentioned Dinah? | They were afraid of a cat being set on them | They did not know what a Dinah was at all | They were listening for the cat's meow | They were sad because Dinah was lost
test: What would have happened if the pebbles had stayed pebbles? | Alice would have had no cake and stayed stuck | Alice would have grown even bigger at once | The house would have fallen down on her | The pebbles would have turned into Bill
test: Grandpa from Somerset: "That were a gurt big puppy, my lover." ("gurt" = great, very.) Why did the puppy seem so huge? | Alice had shrunk very small | The puppy was a giant dog | Alice was looking up a hill | The puppy had eaten a cake
test: True or false: Alice was relaxed and happy playing with the puppy. | False: she was terribly frightened it might trample or eat her | True: she loved dogs and was laughing all the time | True: she knew it was only a tiny toy puppy | False: she was angry because it stole her thimble
test: Mum, sarcastic: "Oh lovely, and NOW she calls it a dear little puppy." Why is that funny? | She was terrified of it a minute ago | The puppy was really a horse | She had never liked dogs at all | The puppy had bitten her hand quite hard before
test: Why does Alice look on top of the mushroom? | She had looked under it, beside it and behind it already | The Caterpillar called her name from the top of it | She wanted to eat a bit of the mushroom right away | The puppy had thrown the stick up on top of it
test: What would a real caterpillar never do that this one does? | Smoke a hookah with its arms folded | Sit on top of a mushroom | Be coloured blue | Crawl slowly about in the long grass
test: Megan from the Valleys: "Alice was tamping, kicking that lizard!" ("tamping" = furious, in Wales.) Was Alice furious when she kicked? | No: she wanted to stop whatever was coming, not to hurt it out of anger | Yes: she was very angry with Bill for breaking the window | Yes: she hated lizards ever since she was very small | No: she kicked Bill only because she was dancing
test: Looking back: in chapter III Alice's talk of Dinah upset her friends by mistake. How does she use Dinah here? | On purpose, as a threat to stop them | By mistake, again upsetting her friends | To make friends with the Lizard | To ask the Rabbit for some help
test: Looking back to chapter I: what was Alice's reasoning about the EAT ME cake? | Larger or smaller, either way she would reach the garden | It could not make her larger, so it would make her smaller | It would make her the right size for the Rabbit's house | It was sure to do nothing, like ordinary cake at home
test: Looking back: the lovely garden was first seen in chapter I. Where was it? | Through a little door in the hall of locked doors | Behind the Rabbit's house past the cucumber-frames | At the top of the well, near the riverbank | Beyond the pool of tears, across the water
test: How has Alice changed since she first met the Rabbit? | She now stands up for herself and threatens back | She is now even more scared and polite to him | She has started working as his housemaid | She has forgotten who the Rabbit even is
test: ★★★ Read carefully: Alice says a cake "can't possibly make me larger". Was she sure, or did she just suppose? | She just supposed: "it must make me smaller, I suppose" | She was sure, because the Rabbit told her so | She was sure, because the cake had a label | She just guessed because it was a pebble
test: ★★★ In the puppy's game it ran "a very little way forwards each time and a long way back". If it ran 1 step forward and 3 steps back each time, where was it after 4 charges? | 8 steps behind where it started | 4 steps ahead of where it started | Back exactly where it started | 12 steps behind where it started
test: ★★★ The voices said "I hadn't to bring but one; Bill's got the other". How many ladders were there? | Two | One | Three | None
exam: Identify the creature dispatched down the chimney of the Rabbit's residence. | Bill, a Lizard | Pat, a gardener | A guinea-pig | The Rabbit himself
exam: Ascertain the transformation undergone by the pebbles subsequent to their arrival upon the floor. | Conversion into small cakes | Conversion into golden keys | Conversion into water | Conversion into mushrooms
exam: It is given that the protagonist threatened to set Dinah upon the assembled company. Determine the immediate consequence thereof. | A dead silence instantly prevailed | The house was forthwith set alight | Pat was despatched to fetch a cat | The Rabbit demanded an apology
exam: Select the response most consistent with the established facts concerning the creature observed upon the summit of the mushroom. | A large blue caterpillar smoking a hookah | A small green frog asleep in the sunshine | The White Rabbit consulting his watch | A cat grinning broadly from ear to ear
